From 6a6baeec9f7724b1791ec0b9eb9eb1e1ba21936b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 22:11:22 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08175/11362] [outer-loop] exp6091: pin the QAT weights the
 live generator actually runs, derived not hardcoded

Operator-caught 2026-08-03. The script hardcoded `gemma-4-31B-it` plus a literal snapshot hash
resolving to `gemma-4-31B-it-Q4_K_M.gguf` -- the NON-QAT build -- while the live ARC generator is
pinned in arc_executable_world_model.py to `gemma-4-31B-it-qat-UD-Q4_K_XL.gguf`. Measuring the
induction wall on a different quantization than the live generator runs is a substrate mismatch,
and the hardcoded snapshot hash is how the drift survived unnoticed.

Now derived from ARC_LIVE_GENERATOR_{REPO_SUBSTR,MODEL_ID,MODEL_FILENAME} and resolved by glob, so
the experiment cannot silently diverge from the live stack again. A missing file is
`blocked_model_not_cached` -- it must NOT fall back to another quantization, which is the exact
failure being corrected.

MTP stays OFF here and that is deliberate, not an omission: ARC_LIVE_GENERATOR_MTP_DEFAULT is "0"
for dev hardware because on a 24 GB card the offload MTP forces costs more throughput than the
~1.4x it returns. MTP-on is the SCORED Kaggle-96GB default. The drafter must also come from the
SAME repo as the target -- a non-QAT drafter with a QAT target is accepted by llama.cpp and
silently degrades, which _resolve_mtp_head() already guards.

Verified: resolves to
models--unsloth--gemma-4-31B-it-qat-GGUF/snapshots/43cc1aeb.../gemma-4-31B-it-qat-UD-Q4_K_XL.gguf

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
